Bitwise operators on the native (arm64/x86-64) lowering.

Apply to unified-rooms after merging `bitwise-operators`:

    git apply docs/integration/arm64-bitwise.patch

The diff is against unified-rooms 2b70b639 and touches only
src/gen/gen_lower_core.zen. It applies unchanged before or after the merge,
but it only compiles once `bitwise-operators` (which adds BinOp.BitAnd..
ShiftRight and UnOp.BitNot) is merged.

What it changes:
- `binary`: maps the operators explicitly (BitAnd->Op.And, BitOr->Op.Or,
  BitXor->Op.Xor, ShiftLeft->Op.Shl, ShiftRight->Op.Shr) instead of the
  `_ => Op.Equal` fallback, which would lower every new operator as equality.
- `binary`: coerces a shift's count to `usize` (U64), as gen_verify requires,
  whatever unsigned type the source count has.
- `unary`: lowers `~x` as `x ^ all-ones` at the operand's width (no new IR
  UnaryOp); a constant `~` folds through `const_int`.
The arm64 and x86 renderers need no change: their existing And/Or/Xor and
Shl/Shr (count >= width gives 0, then canonicalize) already match the
language semantics.

Merge conflict in tests/corpus/gen/scalar_backends (resolve by hand):
- main.expected.darwin: keep unified's deletion.
- main.zen: drop the "{} bitwise refused" println that bitwise-operators adds
  inside the Js/Asm loop (it names `a`, which is not in scope there), and add
  after the "asm bool entry" line:
      println("js bitwise refused {}", refused(env, w, Codegen.Js, "bitwise.zen", Layout.Single).try());
      println("asm bitwise {}", run(env, w, Codegen.Asm, "bitwise.zen", "8\n", 0).try());
- main.expected: after "asm bool entry true" add
      js bitwise refused true
      asm bitwise true
- Take seed/zen.c from either side and regenerate it once the merge builds.

Verified in a scratch detached worktree (2b70b639 + bitwise-operators + this
patch), on macOS arm64: build, run.py --filter bitwise/shift/scalar_backends/
corpus/backends (31/31), and the tests/corpus/bitwise programs compiled with
Codegen.Asm and run natively: 5 of 6 match their .expected byte for byte; the
sixth fails only on `Flags().bits`, an existing native gap ("a field of a
value without record layout"), and matches with that line removed.

